WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.913
A secure upload feature should be
reviewable as a complete workflow.

2
00:00:03.993 --> 00:00:08.229
Review the controller, multipart
configuration, authentication, authorization,

3
00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:12.192
size limits, storage destination, filename
handling, file type validation,

4
00:00:12.272 --> 00:00:15.861
scanning workflow, error handling, cleanup
behavior, download route,

5
00:00:15.941 --> 00:00:17.059
and logging together.

6
00:00:17.139 --> 00:00:20.022
In Java applications, that may involve
Servlet Part APIs,

7
00:00:20.102 --> 00:00:24.045
Spring MultipartFile, JAX-RS multipart
handlers, storage client libraries,

8
00:00:24.125 --> 00:00:26.655
reverse proxy limits, and background
worker code.

9
00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:28.617
Use framework upload APIs carefully.

10
00:00:28.697 --> 00:00:32.816
Prefer streaming or bounded processing for
large files rather than reading entire

11
00:00:32.896 --> 00:00:35.132
untrusted files into memory without
limits.

12
00:00:35.212 --> 00:00:39.213
Keep upload handling separate from
unrelated business logic where practical so

13
00:00:39.293 --> 00:00:40.705
security review is focused.

14
00:00:40.785 --> 00:00:43.668
Store generated identifiers and metadata
deliberately.

15
00:00:43.748 --> 00:00:46.808
Make scanner failure, validation failure,
storage failure,

16
00:00:46.888 --> 00:00:50.948
and cleanup failure visible to the
application instead of burying them in generic

17
00:00:51.028 --> 00:00:51.675
exceptions.

18
00:00:51.755 --> 00:00:55.991
Logging should help operators understand
the lifecycle without exposing sensitive

19
00:00:56.071 --> 00:00:56.601
material.

20
00:00:56.681 --> 00:01:00.387
Useful events include upload accepted,
rejected, scanned, quarantined,

21
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:03.174
released, downloaded, deleted, expired,
and failed.

22
00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:07.078
Logs should avoid file contents, secrets,
authorization headers, cookies,

23
00:01:07.158 --> 00:01:10.983
private signed URLs, storage credentials,
full internal filesystem paths,

24
00:01:11.063 --> 00:01:12.652
and unnecessary personal data.

25
00:01:12.732 --> 00:01:15.850
A correlation ID, file record ID, tenant
ID where appropriate,

26
00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.166
and safe status are usually more useful
than dumping raw details.

27
00:01:19.246 --> 00:01:22.423
Tests should cover allowed files, rejected
files, size limits,

28
00:01:22.503 --> 00:01:25.798
unsupported types, unauthorized users,
tenant boundary checks,

29
00:01:25.878 --> 00:01:29.350
generated filename behavior, path-boundary
checks, cleanup paths,

30
00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.666
scanner failure, quarantine release,
download authorization,

31
00:01:32.746 --> 00:01:34.158
and safe response behavior.

32
00:01:34.238 --> 00:01:37.356
Include upload behavior in pull request
review, CI/CD checks,

33
00:01:37.436 --> 00:01:41.261
deployment review, operational runbooks,
and incident response planning.

34
00:01:41.341 --> 00:01:45.283
A good Java upload implementation is not
just code that accepts multipart data;

35
00:01:45.363 --> 00:01:47.540
it is a controlled lifecycle with
evidence.
